WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Electricity runs everything in an HVAC system. If you don't understand it, you
can't diagnose anything.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Capacitors, contactors, and relays are the three most commonly replaced
electrical components in HVAC. A run capacitor stores and releases energy to
keep motors running efficiently — when it fails, the motor hums but does not

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Run Capacitor: Provides continuous phase shift to keep a motor running
efficiently. Failure causes humming, slow starting, or motor overheating.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Start Capacitor: Provides a brief high-torque boost during motor startup, then
disconnects. Used on hard-start kits.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Dual Run Capacitor: Single capacitor with two sections — one for the compressor
(HERM) and one for the fan motor (FAN), sharing a common terminal (C).

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Bad capacitors are the #1 cause of
no-cooling calls in summer. A technician who can diagnose and replace a
capacitor in 15 minutes is worth their weight in gold to an employer.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: Always replace a capacitor with the EXACT µF rating — wrong
value damages the motor. And — A bulging or leaking capacitor is failed —
replace it even if it still reads in range

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

